We switched our backend to a Node.js Express server that talks to Google Earth Engine and Redis. Please integrate the frontend with this API. Below are the endpoints, payload formats, and example JS code (fetch + map tile usage). Do not attempt to store or use the service-account.json in the frontend — authentication is handled by the backend only.

SERVER BASE URL (replace with production when available):
  http://localhost:3000

CORS: enabled on the server, so the frontend can call the API directly.

Endpoints
---------
1) GET /
   - Health check
   - Response: { message: "Agrisense Node API OK" }

2) POST /api/get-layer
   - Purpose: Request a tile URL (XYZ tile template) for a polygon and layer
   - Body (JSON):
     {
       "polygon": { "type": "Polygon", "coordinates": [ [ [lng, lat], ... ] ] },
       "layer": "ndvi" | "lst" | "elevation" | "soil" | "satellite"
     }
   - Response: { "tile_url": "https://.../tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}?token=..." }

   Example fetch:
   ```js
   async function fetchTileUrl(polygonGeoJson, layer='ndvi') {
     const res = await fetch('http://localhost:3000/api/get-layer', {
       method: 'POST',
       headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
       body: JSON.stringify({ polygon: polygonGeoJson, layer })
     });
     const json = await res.json();
     if (!res.ok) throw new Error(json.error || 'Failed to get tile URL');
     return json.tile_url;
   }
Example usage in Leaflet:

js
Copy code
const tileUrl = await fetchTileUrl(polygon, 'ndvi');
const tileLayer = L.tileLayer(tileUrl, { attribution: 'Agrisense' }).addTo(map);
Example usage in Mapbox GL JS (as a raster source):

js
Copy code
map.addSource('agrisense-tiles', {
  type: 'raster',
  tiles: [ tileUrl ],
  tileSize: 256
});
map.addLayer({ id: 'agrisense-raster', type: 'raster', source: 'agrisense-tiles' });
POST /api/get-stats

Purpose: Enqueue NDVI time-series computation for a polygon (runs in background).

Body (JSON):
{
"polygon": { "type": "Polygon", "coordinates": [ [ [lng, lat], ... ] ] },
"months": 6 // optional, default 6
}

Response: { "jobId": "<uuid>" }

Example fetch:

js
Copy code
async function enqueueStats(polygonGeoJson, months=6) {
  const res = await fetch('http://localhost:3000/api/get-stats', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify({ polygon: polygonGeoJson, months })
  });
  const json = await res.json();
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(json.error || 'Failed to enqueue stats job');
  return json.jobId;
}
GET /api/job/:id

Purpose: Poll job status / retrieve result once complete.

Response examples:

Running: { "id": "123", "state": "active" }

Done: { "id": "123", "state": "completed", "result": [ { "date": "2025-06-01", "value": 0.412 }, ... ] }

Failed: { "id": "123", "state": "failed", "result": null }

Example polling with exponential backoff:

js
Copy code
async function pollJob(jobId, interval=1000, maxAttempts=20) {
  let attempts = 0;
  while (attempts++ < maxAttempts) {
    const res = await fetch(`http://localhost:3000/api/job/${jobId}`);
    const json = await res.json();
    if (json.state === 'completed') return json.result;
    if (json.state === 'failed') throw new Error('Job failed');
    await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, interval * Math.min(8, attempts))); // backoff
  }
  throw new Error('Job timed out');
}
Polygon format notes
The API expects a GeoJSON geometry object (e.g. { "type": "Polygon", "coordinates": [...] }).

Use [lng, lat] ordering (longitude first).

Keep polygons reasonably small for speed; server will reject very large polygons.

Frontend UI suggestions
When requesting a tile, show a loading spinner until the tile layer is added.

For stats jobs, show a "job started" message with a progress spinner and poll /api/job/:id to display results when ready.

Visualize NDVI time series as a line chart (date on x-axis, NDVI on y-axis).

